# Codex Adversarial Review

Target: working tree diff
Verdict: needs-attention

Do not ship yet. G2's single-slash root-relative normalization looks defensible for repo-root semantics, and the 25 fixtures pass, but G1 is not fully closed: the reference-definition parser still misses a valid CommonMark form and also encodes a tab-indentation false positive. Migration should wait for those parser/test fixes.

Findings:
- [high] Valid no-space reference definitions bypass the research guard (tools/validate-research-isolation.py:43)
  `REF_DEF_RE` requires whitespace after the colon via `:\s+`, but CommonMark link reference definitions allow optional spaces/tabs after the colon. A body file can use `[r]:../research/inbox/x.md` plus `[text][r]`; the current extractor returns no target, so a direct research reference can be committed. This is directly contrary to the isolation invariant. CommonMark source: https://spec.commonmark.org/0.30/#link-reference-definitions
  Recommendation: Replace the regex with a small line parser or a corrected regex that allows zero post-colon whitespace and the optional one-line continuation permitted by CommonMark. Add fixtures for `[r]:../research/x.md`, `[r]:
 ../research/x.md`, and title-bearing definitions.
- [medium] Tab-indented reference definitions are treated as CommonMark-valid when they are code blocks (tools/validate-research-isolation.py:42-43)
  The new pattern allows a leading tab in `[ \t]{0,3}` and the tests expect `\t[x]: ../research/y.md` to be detected. CommonMark indentation is column-based; a leading tab advances to a code-block indentation, not a 1-character indentation. This creates false positives in pre-commit for tab-indented code examples and means the G1 CommonMark claim is inaccurate.
  Recommendation: Measure indentation after tab expansion instead of counting raw characters, or restrict the refdef prefix to spaces only. Update the tab-indented fixture to expect no violation, and add cases like ` \t[x]: ...` that also expand to code indentation.
- [medium] Raw HTML links can still point into research without detection (tools/validate-research-isolation.py:85-93)
  `extract_link_targets` only scans markdown inline, angle, and reference-style patterns. CommonMark permits raw HTML, so `<a href="../research/inbox/x.md">draft</a>` or `<img src="/research/foo.png">` renders as a direct reference while producing no detected research target. This is a real bypass if the invariant is '본문에서 research/ 직접 참조 금지', not merely 'three markdown syntaxes only'.
  Recommendation: Either explicitly ban raw HTML in target roots or extract and normalize `href`/`src` attributes from raw HTML blocks/inlines. Add fixtures for double-quoted, single-quoted, and root-relative HTML attributes.

Next steps:
- Fix the reference-definition parser before migration.
- Expand the 25 fixtures with no-space refdefs, tab-expanded indentation, tilde/indented code blocks, and raw HTML href/src cases.
- Re-run `python3 tools/test_validate_research_isolation.py` and `bash tools/validate-research-isolation.sh` after the parser changes.
